\title{Divisibility vs #{\leq}}
\date{2026-04-06T13:48:04Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Remark}
\p{By definition we have ##{(a \mid b) \iff (\exists k \in \Z \st k \cdot a = b).} But consider that we can characterize #{\leq} similarly: ##{(a \leq b) \iff (\exists k \in \N \st k + a = b).} (There is a slight discrepancy because of the need to use #{\N} in the characterization of #{\leq} rather than #{\Z}, but if we forget about negative numbers the discrepancy goes away.) That is, #{\leq} is to addition as #{\mid} is to multiplication. The two relations actually share many important properties in common (both are reflexive and transitive, and antisymmetric when restricted to the natural numbers).}
